this is a production of Cornell  University  grab so I thought I'd get asked a lot  about you know how long does it take to  get a variety out you know what is the  process so I thought I'd walk you  through what we did with Archer my  newest strawberry and it is it can be a  long process so the archer was a  selection I made very early in my career  here at Cornell and it was done very  much in the traditional sense of things  and so you'll see every step was done  through the field on a year-by-year  basis and it takes a long time doing it  that way so we've got some strategies  we've worked on and we'll talk about how  we've been speeding that up but this is  how Archer started so in 99 we made some  some crosses made some hybridizations I  didn't have a lot of information to go  on I hadn't worked on berries for very  long but we had the records from my  patent from the past breeder and we made  some crosses collected the seed in two  thousand that seed got treating got  planted out into the field you just grow  them in the field so we planted them in  rows and let them grow those have to  grow a year go through winter the next  spring we get to see fruit now we've  cleaned that up a little bit we use  plastic we put them on plastic to help  with weed control but the process is  generally the same we put them in the  ground one spring the next spring we get  to see fruit helps as well we use a  little bit of raised beds when you get a  lot of water this year that didn't  happen but last year we had water it was  so high it wet almost over the beds so  from some years we get a lot of rain and  some years yet none but you can't know  that in advance so in 2001 we made the  selection so this is a 2001 selection I  made some notes that day when I selected  it it's a huge king very often the  primary barrier that comes off of  strawberry in the industry they'll call  it the King berry I put very large q  well I use the term queen berry as well  rather than secondary because it's  easier to write but that's not generally  used in the industry and I wrote that it  was firm dat good interior color good  flavor tart that's what I had to go on I  made some notes that day and I picked  three fruit a primary barrier she a  secondary barrier in a tertiary very you  add those up it's almost a hundred grams  of fruit so I calculated if you took a  hundred grams of fruit which is only  three berries per plant on a per acre  basis that's over four thousand pounds  each plant produces more than three  fruit so this is the kind of thing when  we see this kind of thing we're like it  wow we got yield right away because we  got a lot of fruit size it comes off  very quickly and it comes off at once  and for us to have a tertiary Barry and  the 16 grams eyes is a very good berry  in the marketplace 25 will get you about  this big 50 50 50 grams will be about  the size of a plum it's a very large  berry that's larger than what you  normally see we like we'll pick berries  down to about 8 to 10 grams is  acceptable for a commercial product so  that year we had one plant that's all  there is is one plant from that one seed  so whatever runners it produced we  collected those runners routed them and  start and when there's a picture from  that first plant they're not the  prettiest things i'm out there holding  them with one hand taking a picture with  the other so it's really not i'm not a  photographer but it's for me it gets  into my head so i can look back and say  oh I remember that one and that's what  we do to have a picture of kind of a  record as we go you start a plot but it  took a year to make the runners then you  plant them again you got to wait another  year before they fruit again so that  takes a year we planted and then we look  at it for two or three years because we  want to see is it going to live it's  going to have some disease that wipes it  out in the first second year it's a  fruit going to grow or the plants going  to grow or they're going to produce  runners so we collect more runners from  the next plot and then you plant another  plot and you do it again to make sure  because you got a planet in different  locations to make sure you didn't plan  it in the best possible location and it  was great but in a bad location it's  going to die very quickly so we planted  multiple plots at different locations so  that we can see this over time  and then you have to evaluate it for  several years to see how it comes  through winter to see how the plants  grow will they persist in the field will  it have a good yield every year it takes  time to go through this and it can  really take a long time and during that  way most of the selections gets left off  they're found wanting for whatever  reason they're susceptible to some  disease they don't produce enough  runners the fruit quality declines over  time these are things that you see we  take more pictures so we all make sure  we're planting the same plant every time  and that when you it produces fruit the  fruit looks like what we think it's  supposed to look like so each time you  each cycle you're taking pictures to  make sure everything is the same at that  point we decided these are looking good  they're very consistent we're going to  move it into tissue culture because to  take it off the station we needs  disease-free plants I can't collect  stuff from the field and send it out to  growers or to a nursery and it could  have any kind of pathogen on it so we  move it into tissue culture to clean it  up make sure it doesn't have any viruses  make sure it doesn't have buy tops or up  verticillium various fungal pathogens  and that takes can take it a few months  but it could take a year or more to some  some varieties don't tissue culture well  so we got plants we have to check in for  virus we have to produce more plants so  we can put them back out in the field  make sure tissue culture didn't screw it  up sometimes you put things in tissue  culture what you get out of tissue  culture doesn't look like what you put  in and so we have to make sure that we  grow plants make sure they're true to  type make sure they look like what you  expect them to look like all this takes  time and we of course take more pictures  we want to make sure it looks like it's  supposed to look like you have to make  sure you have the same product that you  in the end with of what you started with  so we confirmed trueness of type at that  stage we're like okay we got virus free  plants let's send it to the nursery so  they can propagate it in large numbers  that way we can get it to some of the  growers so in 2014 we sent out sixteen  thousand plus plants to different 11  growers across the state so they could  grow it in a large scale and give me  some feedback on how how it looks for  them how does it look in a commercial  setting up to that point we had 25-foot  plots at the biggest you know so now  they get a thousand plants or two  thousand plants and you can see it on a  van a core a quarter of an acre and  actually see it in a large scale they  evaluated the first year they evaluate  it again the second year if you want to  sit most of the growers will grow it in  a perennial setting and they want to see  it more than one year so all this takes  15 years we get to this point and we're  finally okay it's time to release it and  you're here today it's being released  today it's being announced so it is  available to growers now they won't plan  it until next year but of course we're  taking pictures all along to make sure  that it's the same thing question  yes  so what the growers will pay what the  growers will do or what the nursery will  charge the propagation fee so that the  cost of propagation is offset so it is  not the same price that is its we're not  selling the plants they're offsetting  the cost of propagation it's a semantics  but it is not available to commercial  growers in this kate was only available  to growers of the New York State berry  Growers Association through a  cooperative agreement with Cornell  University that we have with the  Association so we didn't send it to just  any growers we sent it to growers who  were part of this organization that we  had a contract with for doing the  testing and so this was this year this  is fruit I got from a commercial grower  it looked great he was happy with it we  moved forward so that it's now going to  be available and they will plant the  first commercial crop this coming spring  in 2017 of course we won't see that  fruit until 2018 so again there's time  every time you have to grow the plant so  it can really take a long time to get  through it so how do we  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at cornell edu

